WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:10.600
This is the chapter's lab: choose an example, here the course graph G01, then a way to score.

2
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:17.533
Direct weight: G01 has only 6 pairs. A–B and D–E are both 5; by name, A–B comes first.

3
00:00:17.533 --> 00:00:22.933
Switch to shared neighbours: all 10 pairs score. A–B becomes 6.

4
00:00:22.933 --> 00:00:30.021
Click B–D in the log: no direct edge, and through C it gets min(4, 2) = 2.

5
00:00:30.021 --> 00:00:35.888
Switch to the priority graph: by direct weight, the first pair is X–Y.

6
00:00:35.888 --> 00:00:42.888
By shared neighbours, the three triangle pairs score 8, and the first pair becomes P–Q.

7
00:00:42.888 --> 00:00:47.626
Change X–Y to 9: it just takes first place again.

8
00:00:47.626 --> 00:00:55.493
Switch to the tiny netlist with pin-count: the private E–F gets 1 and comes first; A–B has only 0.5.

9
00:00:55.493 --> 00:01:01.493
Compare with net count: without sharing out, A–B scores 2 and beats E–F.

10
00:01:01.493 --> 00:01:07.426
The code lab: your code runs in the browser, with no Python to install.

11
00:01:07.426 --> 00:01:14.626
Run the checks first: none of the three functions is written, so the result is INCOMPLETE.

12
00:01:14.626 --> 00:01:20.759
Task 1: direct weight just ranks the normalised edges. One line is enough.

13
00:01:20.759 --> 00:01:37.789
Task 2: build the adjacency first, then check every unordered pair, including pairs with no direct edge.

14
00:01:37.789 --> 00:01:52.624
Task 3: drop repeated pins in each net, and add w/(|e|−1) to every pair in it.

15
00:01:52.624 --> 00:02:00.424
Run the checks: everything passes, including the original author's priority graph and tiny netlist.

16
00:02:00.424 --> 00:02:09.158
Below, your own function runs on a new graph the exercise never used, compared row by row with the course engine.

17
00:02:09.158 --> 00:02:15.977
Every row matches the engine.

18
00:02:15.977 --> 00:02:23.110
Finally, try the challenges to check you really understand. Results stay in your browser.
